FEATURE: modal-scroll-lock
DATE: 2026-08-03
SCOPE: hold the page with overflow:hidden instead of position:fixed
FOLLOWS: changelog/modal-stacking

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AFFECTED PROJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vUX library (this repository) — the only project affected. No public API changed;
this is an internal change of mechanism with one observable behavioural difference
(see below). Consumer code needs no modification.

  - vUX-modalDisplayer.js   freezePage()/releasePage() rewritten; scroll capture removed
  - doc/modal-displayer.md  mechanism and its consequences documented

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT CHANGED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The page behind an open modal was previously held still by taking the body out of
flow (`position: fixed` plus a negative `top` offset equal to the scroll position).
It is now held with `body { overflow: hidden }`.

Because the document no longer moves, everything that existed to compensate for it
disappears: capturing window.scrollY on open, the negative top offset, the
scrollTo() on close, and the suppress-and-restore of `scroll-behavior` on <html>
that stopped that scrollTo from animating on pages using smooth scrolling.
releasePage() no longer touches <html> at all, and pageState shrank from three
fields to two. The scroll position is now preserved because nothing scrolled,
rather than because it was saved and reapplied.

What is captured and restored is the body's own inline overflow value, so a page
that already set `body { overflow: scroll }` gets that value back rather than having
the property cleared.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LAYOUT DEFECT FIXED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Measured on the example page at a 1280px viewport, opening a modal:

  position:fixed   content width 1217px -> 528.5px   (688px reflow)
  overflow:hidden  content width 1217px -> 1232px    (15px shift)

A body taken out of flow has no containing block width and collapses to
shrink-to-fit, so the entire page behind the modal reflowed dramatically on every
open and back again on every close. This was recorded as a known limitation of the
previous approach and is now fixed.

The remaining 15px is the width of the scrollbar being removed. It is unavoidable
for any scroll lock that hides the scrollbar, and the old mechanism removed the
scrollbar too — so this is not a regression, it is what is left after removing the
much larger one.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BEHAVIOUR CHANGE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`overflow: hidden` blocks user scrolling — wheel, touch, keyboard — but does not
block programmatic scrolling. Consumer code calling scrollTo() can still move the
page behind an open modal. The previous mechanism blocked that too, as a side
effect of the document collapsing to viewport height.

This is documented rather than worked around, on the grounds that suppressing
programmatic scrolling would mean fighting the consumer's own deliberate calls.

Worth noting for anyone reading the tests: this is also why "is scrolling blocked"
has to be verified with a real wheel event rather than with scrollTo(). A scrollTo()
based check fails against a correctly locked page, which is exactly what happened on
the first run of this change before the test was corrected.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Driven in headless Chromium against examples/modal served over HTTP.

  Main suite               71 passed, 0 failed
  Stacking edge suite      16 passed, 0 failed
  Scroll-lock suite         9 passed, 0 failed

The main suite's freeze assertions were switched from body position to body
overflow, and two checks were added that dispatch real wheel events to confirm user
scrolling is genuinely blocked — with one modal open, and with two layers stacked.

The scroll-lock suite covers what this change put most at risk: a tall modal still
scrolls inside its own overlay on a real wheel event while the page behind stays
put; the scroll position is unchanged during and after a modal cycle; a consumer's
own body overflow value is restored rather than cleared; and no inline position or
top residue is left on the body by the old mechanism.

Scrollbar shift was measured separately with Chromium's --hide-scrollbars flag
disabled, since Playwright suppresses scrollbars by default and would have hidden
the artifact being measured — the first measurement reported a 0.0px shift for
exactly that reason and was not trustworthy.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KNOWN LIMITATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

iOS Safari ignores `overflow: hidden` on body for scroll locking, where the
position:fixed technique does work. Mobile Safari is the one environment in which
the previous mechanism was the better of the two. If iOS support becomes a
requirement, the fix is to apply the position:fixed technique on that platform only,
rather than reverting this change for every browser.
